Convolution
-----------

  The SAC convolve_ command is a "discrete" convolution.  It uses two 
  digital-convolution features that may be undesirable:  no scaling of the 
  output by the digitizing interval, and no check on the start time for the 
  pulse.   This program allows for both discrete convolution (as is done by the 
  SAC convolve) and a time-series convolution.

Fortran
~~~~~~~
.. include:: ../../../doc/examples/convolve/convolvef.f
   :code: fortran

C
~
.. include:: ../../../doc/examples/convolve/convolvec.c
   :code: c

Sample Runs for Convolution
---------------------------
 
  After compiling the programs, do the following::

     SAC> fg triangle npts 8 delta 0.02 begin -0.08
     SAC> write triangle_n8_d0.02.sac
     SAC> fg impulse npts 12 delta 0.02 begin 0
     SAC> write impulse_n12_d0.02.sac

  Then::
  
     convolvef
        Usage: convolvef p_name wf_name c_name disc_conv
          where the first three arguments are filenames
          for pulse, waveform, and convolution output.
        If disc_conv is y, it uses a discrete (default SAC) convolution
          and the pulse begin time is set to zero.  This
          reproduces the result one gets for SAC convolve.
        If disc_conv is n, pulse begin time is unchanged
          and the output is multiolied by delta, which is
          what one has in a time-series covolution.

     convolvef triangle_n8_d0.02.sac impulse_n12_d0.02.sac conv_f_n.sac n 
       (or similar entries for convolvec).
     The above run gives a time-series convolution.
     The pulse is symmetric around zero time, so comparing "y" and "n"
       for disc_conv shows an important difference between discrete
       and time-series convolutions.
       
  A PDF document on time-series-convolution_ discusses a more interesting 
  case: both time-domain and discrete convolutions of a pulse with a synthetic
  time series that has a spike at the phase arrival time with Gibbs-phenomenon 
  ringing.
       
Interpolation using cubic splines
---------------------------------

In the pre-digital-data era, data extremes were relatively easy to see because 
the pen or light-beam velocity went to zero.  The interpolation scheme used by 
the SAC interpolate routine uses a method popularized by Wiggins that took 
advantage of that feature so that cycle extrema could be at digitized points.  
With digital date, the extrema are generally not at digitized points, and for  
some studies it is desirable to get a better estimae of the maxima than 
available from Wiggins' interpolation.  The routine below uses a pure 
cubic-spline interpolation published by Forsythe, et al., that may be preferable 
in some studies.

Fortran
~~~~~~~
.. include:: ../../../doc/examples/interpolate/interpolate.f
   :code: fortran

The subroutines called can be found in sac/doc/examples/interpolate. Once you 
have both time_shift.f and interpoolate_subs.f, the compile command is::

    gfortran -o interpolate interpolate.f interpolate_subs.f `sac-config --cflags --libs libsacio`
    
Sample Runs for Cubic-Spline Interpolation
------------------------------------------

  Time series IP07.dHHZ is in directory doc/examples/interpolation.
  It has delta = 0.02::
  
    ./interpolate
      Usage: interpolate filename delta_new lext
        where filename in the input filespec,
        delta_new is the new digitizing interval,
        lext is the number of characters in the extension.
     Output filename is unchanged except extension
        is preceded by i; e.g., bla.z => bla.iz

    ./interpolate IP07.dHHZ 0.0025 4
    
Then::

    SAC> r IP07.dHHZ
    SAC> interpolate delta 0.0025
    SAC> write IP07_sac-int0.0025.dHHZ
    SAC> r IP07.dHHZ IP07.idHHZ IP07_sac-int0.0025.dHHZ
    SAC> cut 0.93 1.04
    SAC> r
    SAC> fileid location ll
    SAC> line increment list 1 2 3
    SAC> title "Top to bottom: raw, Forsythe interp., SAC interp."
    SAC> p2
    SAC> save raw-forsythe-sac.pdf
    SAC> lh depmin depmax
      FILE: IP07.dHHZ  depmin = -1.338549e+02  depmax = 2.504001e+02
      FILE: IP07.idHHZ depmin = -1.386459e+02  depmax = 2.750106e+02
      FILE: IP07_sac-int0.0025.dHHZ depmin = -1.338549e+02  depmax = 2.504001e+02

The plot time-series-interpolation_ shows the initial waveform, the result
using Forsythe interpolation, and the result using SAC interpolation.

Time Shift
----------

Time shift a time series:  There is no function in SAC that does this directly,
but as mentioned in the help file for lowpass_ command, such filters time-shift
the data, and one may want to correct for that time shift.

Fortran
~~~~~~~
.. include:: ../../../doc/examples/time_shift/time_shift.f
   :code: fortran

The subroutines called can be found in sac/doc/examples/time_shift. Once you 
have both time_shift.f and time_shift_subs.f, the compile command is::

    gfortran -o time_shift time_shift.f time_shift_subs.f `sac-config --cflags --libs libsacio`

Sample Runs for Time Shift
---------------------------

The following commands demonstrates how time_shift works::

    SAC> fg seismo
    SAC> write seismo.sac
    SAC> rtr
    SAC> lp co 5.0 np 4
    SAC> p1
    SAC> write seismo_lpco5np4.sac
    SAC> quit

    time_shift seismo_lpco5np4.sac seismo_lpco5np4_ts.sac -0.05
    
Plot time-shift-summary_  shows the original plus the two methods for time-shifting the waveform.
    

